BODY_210 = ("otprint, so nothing else on a site moves. The booths' marks ride the kit's `variant`, as a box truck's fleet does: a level that uses a second look carries one more paint atlas (0.3 MiB measured on 9351), and draws do not change. Over three phones two drew the same look; a hash of place can repeat.\n\n*STATUS: CLOSED 2026-10-08 -- Lot 0.98.2 reads `size[2]` as plan y. Audited both", 'otprint, so nothing else on a site moves. The booths\' marks ride the kit\'s `variant`, as a box truck\'s fleet does: a level that uses a second look carries one more paint atlas (0.3 MiB measured on 9351), and draws do not change. Over three phones two drew the same look; a hash of place can repeat.\n\n**THE MARKS, SMALL AND MOSTLY WEATHER, 2026-10-20** (Zoo 1.117.0, 48fdc71; `patches/zoo_booth_marks/`; `docs/cold_runs/cold_9355/`). The walker read 9351\'s booths on the look-review page: the facing right, the marks not -- "a large shapie squigle doesn\'t look like anything". 1.116.0 drew a tag as a sine wave of discs 3 px (19 mm) across over as much as 74 % of a side panel, laid disc over disc at 0.85 so that it inked all but opaque, and one tile showed on both side panels, so a booth\'s two sides carried the same marks facing each other. 1.117.0 writes words -- JAWN, JEET, ARDY, YOUSE, WOODER, DRAWL, JABRONI, WIT, in stroke letters, each tag in its own lean, width, spacing, shake and tilt, 5 to 8 cm tall, faded to 0.45-0.75, each word and pair of initials once in a booth, a word too long for its panel written smaller and never shortened -- and lays the weather under them. Measured against the unmarked paint rebuilt with the module\'s own helpers, over every form and variant at the default slot and the genome\'s extreme corners: pixels the marks darkened by more than 40 that sit in a 3 x 3 block of them, at most 1 a panel (1.116.0: 92 on the default booth\'s back panel, 230 on its side panel, 987 at a corner); the strongest mark on a white booth 0.75 of the way to black (1.116.0: 1.0). Found on the way and fixed before it shipped: the first painting wrote the same two words on two panels of one booth, and the torn sticker\'s ragged pieces, laid one over another, stacked into a patch as heavy as a tag. **The price, and what it taught about the instrument:** the first read against 9351\'s stored price showed 184 fewer draws, +0.7 to +1.0 ms a heading and 14 MiB less video memory for a change that only adds texture. A control rebuilt in the session -- 9355\'s package with 1.116.0\'s booths swapped back -- read 9355\'s draws at all 53 headings and its p95 within 0.030 ms against a 0.151 ms floor, and 9351\'s own bytes, rebuilt the same way, read 9355\'s draws too. On identical bytes the session had moved the evening draws at two headings (92 each), the frame time (+0.61 ms a heading on the morning package) and the buffer memory (61.6 MB -> 44.6-48.8 MB); texture memory never moved, and it is what the booths cost: 90,168 bytes. What moved the rest is not attributed.\n\n*STATUS: CLOSED 2026-10-08 -- Lot 0.98.2 reads `size[2]` as plan y. Audited both')


def main():
    data = ROADMAP.read_bytes()
    if b"\r\n" in data:
        raise SystemExit("REFUSED: the roadmap is LF and CRLF was found; nothing written")
    text = data.decode("utf-8")
    if all(text.count(new + "\n**%s. " % n) == 1 for n, _, new in ENTRIES) and text.count(BODY_210[1]) == 1:
        print("already applied")
        return 0
    for n, old, _ in ENTRIES:
        if text.count(old) != 1 or text.count(old + "\n**%s. " % n) != 1:
            raise SystemExit("REFUSED: item %s's status block is not there once, above its heading; nothing written" % n)
    if text.count(BODY_210[0]) != 1:
        raise SystemExit("REFUSED: the end of item 210's body is not there once; nothing written")
    for n, old, new in ENTRIES:
        text = text.replace(old, new)
    text = text.replace(BODY_210[0], BODY_210[1])
    for n, _, new in ENTRIES:
        if text.count(new + "\n**%s. " % n) != 1:
            raise SystemExit("REFUSED: item %s's new status does not adjoin its heading; nothing written" % n)
    ROADMAP.write_bytes(text.encode("utf-8"))
    print("roadmap: %d statuses and item 210's paragraph; now tools/roadmap_status.py --write and --check"
          % len(ENTRIES))
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
